# GetSceneObjects.srv — full read-only description of every added scene object.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#   `get_obstacles` is a bare `std_srvs/Trigger` and its payload is
#   newline-joined NAMES and nothing else. So a client could see WHICH objects
#   the planner holds but could never learn an object's type, pose, size or
#   colour — the one thing a scene-editor UI needs in order to show a selected
#   object. Every consumer was forced into the same two bad options: re-derive
#   the geometry it had itself just sent and hope nothing else changed the scene
#   (a client-side cache that disagrees with the server the moment another client
#   touches anything), or show the name and nothing else.
#
#   This reports the geometry the SOLVER actually holds, so the answer is right
#   even for objects placed by the task constructor, the grasp orchestrator, or a
#   plain `ros2 service call` — not just the ones this client added.
#
# READ-ONLY AND CHEAP
#   It reads the ObstacleManager's already-built Scene and rebuilds nothing. It
#   touches no solver and no GPU tensor, so it is safe to poll. Contrast
#   SetJointLocks, which rebuilds the model and every solver (~20 s).
#
# RELATION TO `get_obstacles`
#   `get_obstacles` stays, unchanged, for clients that only want the names (and
#   for the rviz list). This one is the same information plus everything else,
#   as structured fields rather than a string to be parsed.

# Object type constants. These are AddObject.srv's values, repeated rather than
# referenced so a client can decode a reply from this file alone. Kept as
# identical literals on purpose: rosidl cannot import constants from another
# .srv, and a client must not have to keep two files in sync to interpret the
# `types` array below.
int8 CUBOID=0
int8 SPHERE=1
int8 CAPSULE=2
int8 CYLINDER=3
int8 MESH=4

# --- Request (empty: this is a pure query) ---
---

# --- Response ---
bool success
string message

# One entry per object. EVERY array below has exactly the same length and
# index i of each describes the same object — rosidl .srv has no nested
# anonymous record type, so parallel arrays are the idiom here (the alternative
# is a new msg/SceneObject.msg and one more message type to import).
string[] names
int8[] types

# World pose. Note the curobo Scene stores this internally as
# [x, y, z, qw, qx, qy, qz] (w FIRST); it is unpacked into a proper
# geometry_msgs/Pose here, so clients get the conventional x,y,z,w field order.
geometry_msgs/Pose[] poses

# Size, expressed in AddObject.Request's own `dimensions` convention so a record
# from this service can be resubmitted to `add_object` unchanged. Which
# components are MEANINGFUL depends on the type, because curobo's Scene stores
# each shape differently — the unused ones are filled with the used one, never
# with a zero, precisely so the round trip is not rejected:
#
#   CUBOID   -> [dx, dy, dz]                      all three used
#   SPHERE   -> [radius, radius, radius]          only x is meaningful
#   CAPSULE  -> [radius, height, height]          x = radius, y = axis length
#                                                  along the object's local z
#   CYLINDER -> [radius, height, height]          as above
#   MESH     -> [scale_x, scale_y, scale_z]       all three used; the server
#                                                  ignores `dimensions` when the
#                                                  mesh came in as inline
#                                                  vertices/triangles
#
# AddObject rejects any non-positive component, which is why the filler is the
# used value and not 0.
geometry_msgs/Vector3[] dimensions

# rgba as submitted, echoed back.
std_msgs/ColorRGBA[] colors

# Absolute path for a file-backed MESH, empty string for every other type and
# for a MESH that was supplied as inline vertices/triangles. Inline geometry is
# NOT echoed: it can be hundreds of thousands of vertices and belongs on a
# topic, not in a polled query reply. A client that needs it must keep its own
# copy.
string[] mesh_file_paths

# True for the object currently attached to the arm's `attached_object` link.
# At most one entry can be true; the attach link holds a single payload.
#
# An ATTACHED object is still listed here: attach keeps it registered in the
# solver scenes (it is disabled by name, not removed) and only drops it from the
# voxel-map rasterization. That is why the flag exists at all — the name alone
# cannot tell "on the table" from "in the gripper".
bool[] attached